# tools/ales_echo_witness.rish — the feedback delay: the echo that decays (ALES66).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES66.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-191111_fill-ales66-lotus-feedback-delay.md
# Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, sample_max, max_clip, and the one true saturate this reuses); lotus/mix.rye
# (ALES3 — the same saturate reused for a constructive sum, re-proven below).
#
# lotus/echo.rye opens the time-based-effects wing with its primitive, a FEEDBACK delay: y[i] = saturate(x[i] + fb·y[i-delay]), each
# sample the dry input plus a fraction fb = num/den of the OUTPUT one delay earlier, so each echo feeds the next and the train decays.
# The delay line is the clip buffer itself (applied in place, left to right), so an echo needs no carried state — a span split in two
# equals the whole once. The selftest proves: silence stays silence; fb = 0 is the dry identity; a single impulse yields an exact
# decaying echo train (8000 -> 4000 -> 2000 -> 1000 at delay 2, fb 1/2, computed by hand byte-for-byte); the echoes decay and reach
# silence within the clip, never growing; a loud constructive sum saturates to the i16 rail rather than wrapping (ALES3's one true
# saturation, reused); split equals whole byte-for-byte with no carried state; a zero delay refuses BadDelay, a delay past max_clip
# BadDelay, a feedback at unity BadFeedback, a zero denominator BadFeedback, an out-of-range span BadRange. Purely local — no lookahead
# beyond the in-place delay read, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no sample rate.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_echo_witness.rish

say "ales-echo: Language EN — ALES66 opens a whole second wing of the craft. Every dynamics rung so far changes a sample by reading the present — a gain, a threshold crossing, a band. This rung lets a sample RETURN, heard again a while later a little quieter each time: the feedback delay, y[i] = x[i] + fb·y[i-delay]. It is the foundation of the echo, and above it the chorus, the flanger, and the reverb — each one a delay line read a different way. It opens the time-based wing exactly as the sidechain opened the keyed wing: one primitive, then compositions rather than new machinery."
say "ales-echo: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: the delay line IS the clip buffer itself (applied in place, left to right, buf[i-delay] inside the span already holds the output written this pass), so an echo needs no carried state and a span split in two equals the whole once; fb = 0 is the dry identity; a single impulse yields an exact decaying echo train; feedback below unity decays to silence within the clip; and a constructive sum past the rail saturates through the one true saturation rather than wrapping."
say "ales-echo: Lens TAME — one clip of i16 PCM, a u32 span, a u32 delay, a plain u32/u32 feedback fraction; the sum runs in i64 and saturates once, so nothing on the path can overflow; a delay of zero or past max_clip refuses BadDelay, a feedback at or above unity (a runaway that would never decay) or with a zero denominator refuses BadFeedback, a span outside the samples refuses BadRange, all before any write, the clip untouched on refusal; siloed to lotus/, the delay counted in sample indices not milliseconds, no lookahead, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no sample rate."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/echo"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/echo.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-echo: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/echo.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-echo: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-echo: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-echo' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-echo: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-echo line"

# This rung reuses ALES3's one true saturation over its public API — re-prove the mixer still stands green beside it.
let mix = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/mix.rye -femit-bin=lotus/bin/mix && lotus/bin/mix selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-mix' && echo yes"]
assert (trim mix.out) == "yes" else "ales-echo: the ALES3 mixer regressed under the feedback delay"

say "ales-echo: GREEN — the feedback delay opens the time-based-effects wing: y[i] = saturate(x[i] + fb·y[i-delay]), the delay line the clip buffer itself so an echo needs no carried state. Silence stays silence; fb = 0 is the dry identity; a single impulse yields an exact decaying echo train (8000 -> 4000 -> 2000 -> 1000) byte-for-byte; the echoes decay and reach silence within the clip, never growing; a loud constructive sum saturates to the i16 rail rather than wrapping; split equals whole byte-for-byte with no carried state; a zero delay refuses BadDelay, a delay past max_clip BadDelay, a feedback at unity BadFeedback, a zero denominator BadFeedback, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched; the ALES3 mixer stays GREEN. The single-tap slapback, the modulated delay (chorus, flanger), and the multi-tap and real-time forms follow as later rungs on this foundation."
